question_text:
What will be the output of the following C++ code?
int x = 5;
if (x > 3) {
  if (x < 10) {
    cout << "A";
  }
  cout << "B";
}

A. A
B. B
C. AB
D. Nothing

Which statement correctly describes the behavior of the following switch case?
int k = 2;
switch (k) {
  case 1: cout << 1; break;
  case 2: cout << 2; case 3: cout << 3; break;
  default: cout << 0;
}

What does the following code fragment achieve?
for (int m = 0; m < 10; m++) {
  if (m % 2 == 0) continue;
  cout << m;
}

Consider the following code:
char arr[3][5] = {"abc", "def"};
What is the value of arr[0][2]?

What will be printed when x = 15 in the following code?
if x > 20:
    print("A")
elif x > 10:
    print("B")
else:
    print("C")

What does the following code output?
for i in range(3, 6):
    print(i)

What is the output of the following code?
class A:
    def speak(self):
        print("A")

class B(A):
    def speak(self):
        super().speak()
        print("B")

obj = B()
obj.speak()

Which of the following correctly initializes a child class, ensuring proper inheritance of attributes?
class Parent:
    def __init__(self, x):
        self.x = x

class Child(Parent):
    def __init__(self, y):
        # Initialization code here

In the following code, what is the output when print_animal(d) is called?
class Animal:
    def sound(self):
        print("Animal sound")

class Dog(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print("Bark")

def print_animal(animal):
    animal.sound()
d = Dog()

Which of the following is a correct way to override a method in Python?
class Base:
    def method(self):
        print("Base")

class Derived(Base):
    def method(self):\n        # Implementation here

Given the following classes, which statement is true about the method call?
class X:
    def method(self):
        print("X")

class Y(X):
    def method(self):
        print("Y")\n\nobj_x = X()\nobj_y = Y()
